  \ check the typical cooldown period in the help resources to plan this out!**Gathering code modules**\n\nI need to start by gathering the relevant code modules. There's a chance the recipe file for A2C can be found at 'storage/zephyr/submissions/recipe_a2c_...py.' The last capsule mentioned A2C submissions, including details about RNN rollout and other specific models. \n\nI'll check 'storage/zephyr/submissions/' for modules. Given that A2C evaluations exist, I should review evaluation ID 1004 for the submitted code, which has a Score of 91.80. Once I retrieve this code using /execute_action{review 1004}, I can adapt it for GAE λ and advantage normalization in my own storage path.**Creating the A2C submission file**\n\nI’m planning to create a new file named 'storage/zephyr/submissions/recipe_a2c_gae_lambda_sweep.py' since I can write to the storage as part of the Zephyr lineage. The system will automatically create any necessary subdirectories, so that’s good to know. \n\nI’ll code the\
  \ A2C to incorporate GAE advantage and normalization, using α = 0.33 for the gating factor and adjusting parameters like value_loss_coef. I also need to check the station code interface for RL on Sokoban, and I can import pieces from previous submissions, like 'review 1004'.**Managing token cost and tasks**\n\nI need to manage token cost, as I have around 270k available with about 13k already used. First, I’ll execute a series of actions to gather necessary information: \n\n- Reading the research task spec and reviewing code submission 1004.\n- Listing submissions and results under 'storage/zephyr'. \n- Checking probes in 'storage/zephyr/probes' as well.\n\nAfter that, I'll plan to write a new A2C file using GAE with specific configurations. I need to ensure I stay within compute limits while aiming for a high score without exceeding time budgets. I'll focus on one configuration with recommended values to streamline the process."
